+## Review-c8b8a427b8f4d43c — query-rotation repair (2026-10-24)
+
+A retained prior counterexample showed that the version `2.2.0` six-slot
+curriculum placed an unequal occupied-pair conflict only once per three slots.
+When that sole conflict became the query, the plausible canonical-fitting
+`fill_empty_only` rule could survive all remaining demonstrations. A new
+query-rotation regression reproduced the defect at easy band, start 0, query 2;
+its RED receipt is `checks/9536d5aef3f6-rotation-red/result.json`.
+
+Version `2.3.0` alternates ordinary and conflict profiles:
+
+1. `upper_sparse`
+2. `unequal_conflict`
+3. `lower_sparse`
+4. `unequal_conflict_with_diagonal`
+5. `upper_with_diagonal`
+6. `unequal_conflict_dense`
+
+Every cyclic four-example window now contains two unequal conflicts, so holding
+out any one query leaves at least one decisive conflict demonstration. The new
+dense conflict profile preserves the same rule while varying which side supplies
+additional markers. Conflict-location tests derive all conflict slots instead of
+assuming two fixed indices. The canonical Racket authority was not edited.
+
+Final generator SHA-256:
+`6c815b5df302aa426dbf53a9fdb88211fb9b73fc2747e5f79741b5760f62ee4e`.
+`checks/9536d5aef3f6-tests/result.json` records 11/11 tests passing.
+The reusable `workspace/rotation_probe.py` checked 144 rotations and 144
+protected-Racket labels across three bands and twelve starts, with zero
+ambiguities and zero Python/Racket mismatches; every held-out rotation retained
+at least one conflict demonstration. This is a finite schedule/rival audit, not
+a universal learnability proof.
